subscribe now, so you don't miss the next

50
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:45,192
one.

51
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:47,363
So what did he expect the market to

52
00:01:47,433 --> 00:01:48,315
do next?

53
00:01:48,367 --> 00:01:49,354
Part two.

54
00:01:49,714 --> 00:01:51,037
The bankers' game.

55
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:54,160
Damodaran says bankers have one job in an

56
00:01:54,242 --> 00:01:55,066
IPO:

57
00:01:55,496 --> 00:01:57,562
price it so the stock pops on day

58
00:01:57,643 --> 00:01:58,073
one.

59
00:01:58,503 --> 00:02:02,136
He expected an open around $90 to $95.

60
00:02:02,496 --> 00:02:04,842
But he said the opening price is irrelevant.

61
00:02:05,271 --> 00:02:07,175
What matters is where it closes,

62
00:02:07,373 --> 00:02:09,962
because insiders could start selling from day one.

63
00:02:10,635 --> 00:02:12,028
Same door, he said,

64
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:14,292
with people trying to get in and people

65
00:02:14,327 --> 00:02:15,441
trying to get out.

66
00:02:16,219 --> 00:02:17,404
His line in the sand:

67
00:02:17,636 --> 00:02:19,888
if it fell to $71 or

68
00:02:20,248 --> 00:02:22,872
$72, it would start to get interesting for

69
00:02:22,941 --> 00:02:23,232
him.

70
00:02:24,474 --> 00:02:25,333
Here's what happened.

71
00:02:25,495 --> 00:02:29,106
Anthropic opened around $111 and closed its first

72
00:02:29,153 --> 00:02:30,801
day near $97.

73
00:02:31,231 --> 00:02:32,694
Four trading days later,

74
00:02:32,775 --> 00:02:34,586
it fell below $83.

75
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:37,140
By Wednesday, it was near $79,

76
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:39,253
closing in on his $77.

77
00:02:39,613 --> 00:02:40,635
But even at his number,

78
00:02:40,658 --> 00:02:41,970
he wasn't rushing to buy.

79
00:02:42,284 --> 00:02:43,515
It needs to drop a lot,

80
00:02:43,549 --> 00:02:45,953
he said. And the reason has nothing to

81
00:02:46,034 --> 00:02:47,067
do with AI.

82
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:48,015
Part three.

83
00:02:48,375 --> 00:02:49,571
Designed in hell.

84
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,857
Most tech founders keep control with special voting

85
00:02:52,903 --> 00:02:53,437
shares.

86
00:02:53,867 --> 00:02:55,237
Anthropic went further.

87
00:02:55,597 --> 00:02:58,046
Damodaran describes a single founder share,

88
00:02:58,360 --> 00:02:59,776
held by seven founders,

89
00:03:00,008 --> 00:03:02,702
carrying 50.01% of the votes.

90
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:05,755
He calls the structure one designed in hell

91
00:03:05,825 --> 00:03:06,777
for shareholders.

92
00:03:07,264 --> 00:03:08,507
His comparison:

93
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:11,990
the golden shares governments kept when they privatized

94
00:03:12,036 --> 00:03:14,474
companies in the 1980s and 90s,

95
00:03:14,741 --> 00:03:16,332
like in Margaret Thatcher's Britain.

96
00:03:16,761 --> 00:03:17,644
One share,

97
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:20,604
but a veto over the big decisions.

98
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:23,425
And he sees a twist the founders may

99
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:24,633
not have thought through.

100
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:27,860
If AI ever causes a scandal,

101
00:03:28,023 --> 00:03:30,833
an activist government could target that one share.

102
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:33,468
Take it, pay the price of an ordinary

103
00:03:33,549 --> 00:03:37,694
share, and suddenly control the company's biggest decisions.

104
00:03:38,182 --> 00:03:40,190
The founders could fight that in court,

105
00:03:40,225 --> 00:03:40,933
he says.

106
00:03:41,363 --> 00:03:42,895
But it paints a target.

107
00:03:43,383 --> 00:03:45,426
There's a practical problem too.

108
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,631
If the business needs to change course,

109
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:50,999
who can make the founders listen?

110
00:03:51,672 --> 00:03:54,087
Maybe only a falling stock price,

111
00:03:54,447 --> 00:03:56,595
which he calls a blunt instrument.

112
00:03:57,082 --> 00:03:59,625
So what would make the business itself work?

113
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:00,548
Part four.

114
00:04:00,977 --> 00:04:02,022
The one number.

115
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:05,551
Damodaran's first rule of corporate finance:

116
00:04:06,039 --> 00:04:08,431
young companies that aren't making money shouldn't borrow.

117
00:04:08,791 --> 00:04:10,300
Anthropic, he says,

118
00:04:10,532 --> 00:04:12,552
broke it by signing hundreds of billions in

119
00:04:12,634 --> 00:04:15,304
compute commitments without the earnings to back them.

120
00:04:15,733 --> 00:04:17,440
What buys time is cash.

121
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:19,054
After the IPO,

122
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:20,876
he expects about $200 billion,

123
00:04:21,364 --> 00:04:22,955
enough to cover the next two or three

124
00:04:22,989 --> 00:04:24,162
years of those bills.

125
00:04:24,522 --> 00:04:27,181
But only if revenue roughly doubles every year

126
00:04:27,227 --> 00:04:28,109
in the near term.

127
00:04:28,597 --> 00:04:31,221
That's the one number he says drives everything.

128
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:33,055
If revenue comes in,

129
00:04:33,136 --> 00:04:34,204
profits come,

130
00:04:34,564 --> 00:04:36,015
the commitments get paid,

131
00:04:36,097 --> 00:04:37,374
and the risk goes away.

132
00:04:37,803 --> 00:04:38,721
If it doesn't,

133
00:04:38,918 --> 00:04:40,567
everything else falls apart.

134
00:04:41,135 --> 00:04:43,306
Host Ed Elson pointed out that growth was

135
00:04:43,376 --> 00:04:47,230
already slowing, from 47% a quarter toward 15

136
00:04:47,311 --> 00:04:48,438
to 25%.

137
00:04:48,832 --> 00:04:50,771
Damodaran blames competition:

138
00:04:51,224 --> 00:04:54,417
OpenAI underpricing, and cheaper open models.

139
00:04:55,264 --> 00:04:56,832
Most businesses don't need Claude,

140
00:04:56,878 --> 00:04:57,459
he says.

141
00:04:57,981 --> 00:04:58,979
It's overkill.

142
00:04:59,815 --> 00:05:02,555
His advice: build models powerful enough,

143
00:05:02,799 --> 00:05:03,961
but not too powerful.

144
00:05:04,297 --> 00:05:06,155
And even if AI helps cure diseases,

145
00:05:06,178 --> 00:05:06,875
